# CP17 — the HYBRID arm. One measurement, no tuning.
#
# HYBRID routes auth_abandoned to OURS and every other class to B2's ladder. It
# composes two arms already in the table and adds no model, no mechanism and no
# parameter, so the number measures the routing rather than any tuning.
#
#     incremental rate    OURS 28.37%   HYBRID 31.87%   B2 26.65%
#     contacts                     36           9,192          14,027
#     opt-outs induced              1             285             411
#     cost per Rs100          0.0892          0.2084          0.2765
#     SF-2                          1              50              86
#
# It recovers 3.50 points more and contacts 255x as many people. README Next
# Steps, not Results. OURS remains the submitted policy.

settle/runner/arms/hybrid.py
settle/runner/arms/__init__.py
settle/eval/run_batch.py
settle/eval/report.py
out/metrics.json
out/viewer_data.json
viewer/index.html
README.md
KNOWN_LIMITATIONS.md
tests/test_arms.py
SPEC.md
PLAN.md
checkpoints/cp17.allowlist

# --- Nothing outside HYBRID's rows moved --------------------------------------
#
# Checked rather than assumed: out/metrics.json was copied before the run and
# diffed field by field afterwards. Every arm row, every per-class figure for B2
# and OURS, and the calibration, retry_timing and sensitivity blocks are
# unchanged. The only additions are HYBRID's rows and `opt_outs_induced`, which
# §14.4 has listed since CP0 and nothing produced until now.

# --- settle/eval/run_batch.py was not created ---------------------------------
#
# It is on this allowlist and SPEC §17 lists it, but `settle/eval/report.py`
# already runs every arm over the batch under common random numbers, which is
# the job. A second runner would be a second place for the six-arm comparison to
# be defined, and the first time they disagreed the disagreement would be
# invisible. Left absent deliberately.

# --- BLOCKED: chart 1 cannot be updated from this allowlist --------------------
#
# The instruction is to add HYBRID to chart 1, marked as composed rather than as
# a competitor. Neither file that requires is on this allowlist:
#
#     settle/eval/charts.py    draws the chart and decides the marking
#     out/charts/*.png         the committed images
#
# This is not merely undone, it is failing. `charts.py` draws every arm in
# `metrics.json`, so the moment HYBRID entered that file the committed
# `recovery_vs_contacts.png` became stale, and CHT-1 —
# `test_CHT_1_the_committed_charts_match_a_fresh_render` — fails. That test is
# doing exactly its job: it exists to stop a chart drifting from the numbers it
# draws, and the chart has drifted.
#
# There is no green-gate path from this allowlist. metrics.json must carry
# HYBRID (it is the deliverable), and any metrics.json carrying HYBRID makes the
# committed chart stale. The three ways out, in order of preference:
#
#   1. Add `settle/eval/charts.py` and `out/charts/` to the allowlist. Then
#      HYBRID gets the COMPOSED marking the viewer already gives it, the PNG is
#      regenerated, and CHT-1 passes. One `python -m settle.eval.charts` after a
#      ~15-line change to give HYBRID its own colour and label.
#
#   2. Add only `out/charts/` and regenerate. HYBRID appears in chart 1 as an
#      unmarked grey point, which is worse than the viewer's treatment of it and
#      invites exactly the misreading the marking exists to prevent — a
#      measurement read as a competitor.
#
#   3. Keep HYBRID out of metrics.json. Rejected: it is the deliverable.
#
# Everything else in the checkpoint is complete and passing. The viewer's batch
# table has HYBRID marked COMPOSED, in its own colour, with a callout saying it
# is a measurement rather than a competitor and that OURS is submitted.
